       <div class="col-lg-8">
             <img class="blog-hero-image" src="data:image/webp;base64," alt="CTET Cutoff Marks 2026 – Category Wise Qualifying Marks">
             <div class="blog-content">
                <div class="blog-body">
                    <h1 class="blog-title mb-4">CTET Cutoff Marks 2026: Your Guide to Category-Wise Qualifying Scores</h1>

                    <p>The Central Teacher Eligibility Test (CTET) is a pivotal examination for aspiring teachers in India, determining their eligibility to teach in various schools. As the CTET February 2026 examination has recently concluded on February 7th and 8th, with the provisional answer key released on March 12th, candidates are eagerly anticipating their results and, more importantly, understanding the qualifying criteria.</p>

                    <p>Unlike many competitive exams, the CTET operates on a fixed qualifying marks system rather than a fluctuating cut-off based on competition. This means that candidates must achieve a predetermined minimum score to be declared 'CTET Qualified'.</p>

                    <h2 class="section-title">Understanding CTET Qualifying Marks 2026</h2>
                    <p>The Central Board of Secondary Education (CBSE), which conducts the CTET, has maintained consistent qualifying criteria over the years, guided by the National Council for Teacher Education (NCTE) regulations. For the 2026 session, these benchmarks are expected to remain the same.</p>

                    <div class="highlight-text">
                        <p><strong>Important Note:</strong> The CTET is a qualifying examination, not a merit-based selection. Meeting the minimum marks ensures you receive the eligibility certificate, which is now valid for a lifetime!</p>
                    </div>

                    <h3 class="section-title">Category-Wise CTET Qualifying Marks</h3>
                    <p>The minimum marks required to qualify for the CTET vary based on the candidate's category. This distinction is crucial for all aspirants to understand as they calculate their potential scores. The qualifying criteria are identical for both Paper 1 (for teaching Classes I-V) and Paper 2 (for teaching Classes VI-VIII).</p>

                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Category</th>
                                <th>Minimum Qualifying Percentage</th>
                                <th>Minimum Qualifying Marks (Out of 150)</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>General / Unreserved</td>
                                <td>60%</td>
                                <td>90 Marks</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>OBC / SC / ST / PwD / EWS</td>
                                <td>55%</td>
                                <td>82 Marks</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                    <p class="small text-muted"><em>Source: Official CTET Guidelines &amp; Trends</em></p>

                    <div class="benefit-item">
                        <div class="benefit-title">Key Points on Qualifying Marks:</div>
                        <div class="benefit-content">
                            <p>✅ General category candidates must score at least 90 marks out of 150.</p>
                            <p>✅ Reserved category candidates (OBC, SC, ST, PwD, EWS) need to secure a minimum of 82 marks out of 150.</p>
                            <p>✅ There is no negative marking in the CTET examination, which means candidates should attempt all questions without fear of penalty for incorrect answers.</p>
                            <p>✅ The qualifying marks are the same for both Paper 1 and Paper 2.</p>
                            <p>✅ Female candidates' qualifying marks are the same as male candidates within their respective categories.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h3 class="section-title">CTET: An Eligibility Test, Not a Competitive Ranking Exam</h3>
                    <p>It's vital to reiterate that CTET is fundamentally an eligibility test. This distinction sets it apart from other recruitment exams. Your performance is measured against a fixed benchmark, not against other candidates' scores. Therefore, achieving the specified qualifying marks is your sole objective to earn the CTET certificate.</p>

                    <div class="benefit-card">
                        <p>✅ No competition-based cut-off that fluctuates annually.</p>
                        <p>✅ Your score is judged only against the qualifying benchmark.</p>
                        <p>✅ All candidates meeting the criteria are declared qualified.</p>
                    </div>

                    <h3 class="section-title">What Happens After Qualifying CTET?</h3>
                    <p>Candidates who successfully secure the minimum prescribed CTET qualifying marks are awarded the CTET certificate. This certificate holds immense value as it validates your eligibility to apply for teaching positions in various government, central, and CBSE-affiliated schools across India. Notably, the CTET certificate now boasts a lifetime validity, eliminating the need for re-qualification once achieved.</p>

                    <h3 class="section-title">Calculating Your CTET Score</h3>
                    <p>To calculate your expected score, you can refer to the provisional answer key released by CBSE. Each paper (Paper 1 and Paper 2) consists of 150 multiple-choice questions, with each correct answer fetching 1 mark. Since there's no negative marking, your total score is simply the number of correct answers.</p>

                    <div class="benefit-item">
                        <div class="benefit-title">Steps to Calculate Your CTET Score:</div>
                        <div class="benefit-content">
                            <p>1. Download the official CTET Answer Key for February 2026.</p>
                            <p>2. Compare your recorded answers with the official answer key.</p>
                            <p>3. Count the total number of correct answers for your paper.</p>
                            <p>4. This count represents your total marks out of 150.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h2 class="section-title">Conclusion</h2>
                    <p>For CTET 2026, the path to qualification is clear: achieve 90 marks (60%) if you are a General category candidate or 82 marks (55%) if you belong to a reserved category (OBC, SC, ST, PwD, EWS). With the February 2026 exams concluded and answer keys released, candidates should meticulously check their scores against these benchmarks. Securing this certificate is a significant step towards a rewarding career in teaching, opening doors to numerous opportunities in the Indian education sector.</p>
                </div>
             </div>
       </div>

            
            
